// Public SearchTree trait — a behavioural view over the SAME wbt_set value that implements
// OrderedSet (Layer 5 terminal trait). There is no separate core or storage: the receiver is the
// Node shape, spelled in every ref? to a node so rec binds to it: (ItemType, int64, ref?(L, normal, rec), ref?(L, normal, rec)).
// wbt_set record, updates go through wbt_set@empty/insert/delete/from_list/from_sorted, and the
// returned value keeps implementing both SearchTree and OrderedSet.
// Authority: design_documents/Phase1_TODOs/data_structure_designs/search_tree_trait.md §3.
//
// Required: contains_key (direct WBT search, wbt_set@contains; it does not fold, design §3) and
// compare_item (the comparator captured by the tree, validated at every call). The design declares
// no provided methods, so there is no provided block.
// Deviations forced by the compiler (binaries/silica-compiler of 2026-09-18):
//   1. The impls take the inline wbt_set record (as OrderedSet does). A `SearchTree[ItemType,
//      mem(normal)]` binding is accepted only for a wbt_set@empty/1 constructor call; every other
//      result (singleton, insert, delete, from_list, from_sorted) is bound with the inline record type.
//   2. A parameter declared SearchTree[ItemType, mem(normal)] (the "algorithm that requires only
//      SearchTree" of design §6) accepts the tree returned by a persistent update (design §2/§4: the
//      same concrete type), and a `t: SearchTree[...] <- r.set` binding accepts it too. This was
//      formerly an open defect (E2003); fixed, covered by
//      trials/ordered_data_structures/search_tree_collections/st_parameter_accepts_updated_tree.
//   3. mem(normal) only, as in every other Phase 1 module.
// Both views of one value agree by construction: SearchTree@contains_key and OrderedSet@contains are
// the same wbt_set@contains, and both compare_item impls are wbt_set@apply_compare_item. A user unit
// that cases on compare_item's result lists :less, :equal, :greater in that order where they first
// appear (atoms are numbered per unit; see the brodal_okasaki_compare header).

use wbt_set;

export trait SearchTree;
export contains_key/2;
export compare_item/3;

required {
    fn compare_item(tree: SearchTree, a: ItemType, b: ItemType) -> :less | :equal | :greater;
    fn contains_key(tree: SearchTree, key: ItemType) -> boolean;
}

impl fn compare_item(
    tree: {
        root: ref?(L, normal, (ItemType, int64, ref?(L, normal, rec), ref?(L, normal, rec))),
        compare_item: fn(ItemType, ItemType) -> :less | :equal | :greater,
        region: region(L, normal),
        specialization_key: int64,
        compare_item_ordering_bundle: int64
    },
    a: ItemType,
    b: ItemType
) -> :less | :equal | :greater {
    wbt_set@apply_compare_item(tree, a, b)
}

impl fn contains_key(
    tree: {
        root: ref?(L, normal, (ItemType, int64, ref?(L, normal, rec), ref?(L, normal, rec))),
        compare_item: fn(ItemType, ItemType) -> :less | :equal | :greater,
        region: region(L, normal),
        specialization_key: int64,
        compare_item_ordering_bundle: int64
    },
    key: ItemType
) -> boolean {
    wbt_set@contains(tree, key)
}
